<file path="docs/capabilities.md">
# AI capability runtime

Write the intent as an Aithon expression. Python values and objects remain live:

```python
from aithon import Audio
meeting = Audio("meeting.mp3")
text: str = ถอดเสียงจาก meeting
```

Valid Python runs normally. Capability selection only happens inside an AI statement or recovery. Intent resolution and planning share the existing agent response; execution of a known plan does not add an orchestration model call per step. A reasoning, transcription or generation step can itself involve a model request. Data-dependent plans may require further agent responses.

## Configure one project

Existing `version = 1` configs and provider plugins with `complete(messages, tools)` continue to work. They provide reasoning; they do not silently gain media capabilities. Minimal existing configuration remains:

```toml
version = 1
default_profile = "default"
[profiles.default]
provider = "openai-compatible"
base_url = "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
model = "YOUR_REASONING_MODEL"
api_key = "YOUR_KEY"
```

For capabilities, copy `examples/capabilities/aithon.toml.example` to `aithon.toml` in that example directory and replace keys/model IDs. Run `uv run aithon --trace-plan examples/capabilities/documents.py`. There is still just one `default` profile. Named providers hold credentials; routes select the provider and model per capability. Delete unused routes/providers. Keys may be literal `api_key` or an `api_key_env` environment variable name. No global setup is necessary, and project credentials are not merged with global config.

Each route array is ordered. Fallback occurs only after an explicitly retryable rejection, such as HTTP 429. Connection failure or a server error can mean an expensive job was already accepted: Aithon does not automatically resubmit. Missing routes, unsupported adapter capabilities, permission errors and malformed responses are explicit errors. Model IDs are explicit; the adapter checks protocol capabilities locally, while the provider validates model-specific availability/modalities. There is no speculative model-discovery request per statement.

Video routes use `modes = ["understand"]` or `["generate"]` to select the appropriate model without guessing from its name. A text-only embedding model cannot embed an image; configure a multimodal embedding model for visual search. Every query/index pair must use the same embedding space, revision and dimension. Rebuild an index explicitly when changing these.

## Capabilities and assets

| Capability | Adapter | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| reasoning | OpenRouter, Gemini, legacy reasoning | str |
| vision | Gemini | str |
| document_understanding | Local TXT/Markdown; Gemini PDF/multimodal | source-tagged chunks, or str for a prompted answer |
| embedding | OpenRouter text; Gemini text/media | Embeddings |
| indexing | Local SQLite | VectorIndex |
| semantic_search | Local exact cosine search | hits with text/source/score/object |
| reranking | OpenRouter | original records in relevance order |
| speech_to_text | Gemini | str |
| text_to_speech | Gemini Interactions | Audio |
| image_generation / image_editing | Gemini | Image |
| video | Gemini understanding / Veo generation | str / Video |

`Image`, `Audio`, `Video`, `Document` and `VectorIndex` are Python objects represented to the agent by handles. Asset bytes are loaded at the provider boundary, not embedded in the planner context. Relative paths resolve against the project directory. Large Gemini inputs use lazy resumable uploads with in-process URI reuse; smaller ones use inline data only in the capability request.

Generated assets have `.path`, `.save(destination)` and `os.fspath()` support. `.save()` refuses to overwrite a file. Default output lives in `.aithon/artifacts/` with unique names. TTS raw PCM is wrapped as WAV. Local text extraction keeps line locations; PDF extraction asks the model for page-tagged chunks, so PDF page references remain model-produced and should be checked for critical uses.

Local indexes persist vector/text/source snapshots. Search preserves the identity of catalog objects attached to the index in the current execution. After restarting Python, only persisted text/source records are available; arbitrary Python objects are never pickled or recreated automatically.

## Directives and output constraints

```python
# aithon: capability="semantic-search" provider="local" prompt="คืน object เดิม"
results = หาเอกสารที่เกี่ยวข้องกับคำถามนี้
```

Existing `profile`, `prompt`, and `begin`/`end` regions retain their lexical scope. Capability names accept hyphens or underscores. A capability directive requires that capability in the plan, while permitting prerequisite steps. A provider directive restricts its route; by itself it applies to the output step's capability. It cannot introduce credentials or unconfigured routes. CLI `--force-profile` still wins over source profile selection.

Annotations are enforced across project Python and AI boundaries; see [type safety](type-safety.md). AI assignment annotations are propagated as output constraints and checked before binding. Supported annotations include builtins, list/dict/tuple/set, unions, Literal, dataclasses, TypedDict, and custom classes via `isinstance`. Annotation expressions are not evaluated and do not construct objects. `aithon.type_constraints.register_validator(Class, validator)` adds a trusted custom validator. Unsupported annotations fail explicitly. An invalid output is returned to the existing bounded agent loop for repair; already completed plan steps remain available.

## Cache, permissions and recovery

`.aithon/runtime.sqlite` stores project-local caches, indexes and job manifests. Cache keys include content snapshots, parameters, prompt/output constraints, provider endpoint/model/revision, and adapter version. Cache TTL is conservatively 24 hours for all routes; configure a revision when available. Text extraction, transcription, image analysis and embeddings cache by default. Content-addressed indexes reuse identical vector/source snapshots. Generative outputs are fresh unless a plan explicitly requests cache reuse. Custom/live objects and Python side effects are not serialized into the cache.

Permissions: `read_asset`, `network`, `generate_file`, `write_filesystem`, `execute_code`. Checks happen before plan execution and before cached results are returned. Cache use is disabled when cache filesystem permissions are absent. All are allowed by default for compatibility. A denied capability stops execution rather than inviting the agent to bypass it through another tool.

This is a **trusted in-process runtime**, not a sandbox. Python code and unrestricted eval/exec have process privileges and can bypass these checks. Use OS isolation for untrusted source. Ordinary Python remains outside capability permission enforcement.

Plans validate routes, fields, literal input types, permissions and the entire dependency graph before execution. Values obtained from dependencies are checked when available. Pure independent steps may run concurrently. If one fails, other already-started steps may still finish; their results remain available. Reuse successful step IDs unchanged in a corrected plan. Aithon blocks repeated uncertain submissions and never rolls back completed side effects. Media failures can be inspected through the agent’s `jobs` and `resume_job` tools without another submission. The existing `complete` / `retry` / `reraise` recovery contract is retained.

## Inspect execution and video jobs

- `--explain`: static only, no AI calls, no program execution. Runtime-dependent plans, cost and cache state are reported as unknown.
- `--trace-plan`: actual capability route, cache status, elapsed time and provider usage/cost, when supplied. No fabricated prices.
- `--stats`: retains agent metrics and adds capability events. Pure-step worker requests contribute to request-byte totals.
- `aithon jobs list --script path/to/main.py`: list persisted operations.
- `aithon jobs resume OPERATION_ID --script path/to/main.py`: poll/download an existing video job using its configured provider/model; does not submit another job or resume the Python stack.

Video assignments wait for their job. Timeout leaves an operation ID for resume. Ctrl-C requests provider cancellation when supported and records the state; cancellation is best effort.

## Extending and testing

`CapabilityProvider` defines `capabilities()` and `invoke(CapabilityRequest, context) -> CapabilityResult`. Register providers with `aithon.providers`. Additional `CapabilitySpec` factories use the `aithon.capabilities` entry-point group. Keep schemas, validation, permissions and effect/cache policies explicit.

`run_plan` takes steps `{id, capability, params, provider?, cache?}` and an output reference. References are `{"$ref":"step", "path":[...]}`, `{"$handle":"object-1"}` or `{"$binding":"python_name"}`. Use `{"$asset":"shoe.jpg"}` for media embedding inputs; plain strings are embedded as text. Paths traverse dict/list values; `"*"` projects over a list, such as `["*", "object"]` to return original catalog objects. A plan result can be returned with `finish(result_from=...)` in the same agent response.

Run offline checks with `uv run python -m unittest discover -s tests -v`. Native HTTP contracts use fixtures; paid live tests are opt-in. Set `AITHON_LIVE_CAPABILITY_CONFIG` to your config path and `AITHON_LIVE_CAPABILITY_CASES` to a JSON array of `{capability, params}` objects to exercise selected real routes. Generated media incurs the configured provider's charges; ordinary tests never call those APIs.

API references: [OpenRouter embeddings/rerank](https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/evaluate-and-optimize/rag), [Gemini embeddings](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/embeddings), [image generation](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/image-generation), [speech](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/speech-generation), [files](https://ai.google.dev/api/files), [Veo](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/veo).
</file>

<file path="docs/type-safety.md">
# Declared types are runtime contracts

Aithon checks explicit annotations throughout project code, including ordinary Python. It sends the same contract to the AI as an output schema. No special base class or framework is required.

```python
from typing import Annotated, Literal, TypedDict

class TicketAnalysis(TypedDict):
    category: Literal["availability", "performance", "security", "billing", "feature_request", "other"]
    severity: Literal["critical", "high", "medium", "low"]
    summary: Annotated[str, "สรุปปัญหาภาษาไทยไม่เกิน 1 ประโยค"]
    reason: Annotated[str, "เหตุผลสั้น ๆ ว่าทำไมถึงเลือกระดับนี้"]
    recommended_team: str

analysis: TicketAnalysis = วิเคราะห์ ticket ปัจจุบัน
```

`analysis` is a real Python dict. The model receives its fields, required keys, literal choices and descriptions, even when the type definition is outside the nearby source window. `Annotated` text guides the AI; it is not an executable assertion about sentence length or language. Use a registered validator for additional enforceable conditions.

## Enforcement

- Annotated assignments and later assignments to the same binding are checked. Invalid simple assignments are rejected before replacing the value.
- Function arguments, positional-only/keyword-only arguments, `*args`, `**kwargs`, explicit returns and implicit `None` returns are checked. Async functions use the same rules.
- The return value is checked again after `finally`, so cleanup cannot silently invalidate a return that was already checked.
- Typed class fields are checked on direct attribute assignment. Dataclass values are checked without conversion to dictionaries or reconstruction. Nested registered project instances are checked at execution boundaries.
- Mutable containers are checked deeply. Mutations through aliases are detected at statement boundaries. Enclosing scopes and globals are included; closures preserve referenced local annotation names.
- Generator functions check each yielded value, sent value and final return. Async generators and `yield from` retain their control-flow protocols.
- AI `evaluate`, `execute`, binding writes, terminal results and recovery replacement values use the same checker. Invalid AI output can be repaired within the bounded agent loop before assignment; completed capability side effects are retained.
- Project imports are instrumented too. Unannotated bindings remain dynamic; correctly typed Python does not call AI.

Expected types reach direct AI expressions in annotated assignments, later assignments to declared names, function returns, yields and arguments of locally declared functions. Runtime boundary checks still apply when an expected type cannot be inferred for the planner.

## Type forms

Contracts support primitives, `None`, `Any`, unions/Optional, Literal values, Annotated descriptions, nested list/dict/set/frozenset/tuple, concrete Sequence/Mapping values, TypedDict with Required/NotRequired, dataclasses, nominal classes, Self, type parameters, TypeVar constraints, NewType's underlying runtime type, type[T], and recursive/generic Python 3.14 type aliases. Generic class fields are checked after substituting supplied type arguments.

Primitive checks are strict: no string-to-number conversion, and bool does not pass an int contract. `Any` is an explicit escape from value checking. Bare containers have unconstrained elements. `Final` bindings reject reassignment; `ClassVar` direct writes must target the class.

Annotations are interpreted rather than passed to eval. Aithon uses Python 3.14 string-format annotation introspection and resolves names in their execution/definition scopes. Custom annotation machinery and custom validators are trusted Python code, not sandboxed code.

`aithon.type_constraints.register_validator(Class, validator)` supplies a custom runtime predicate. A class validator returns true for a valid value. It can also provide the structural check for a Protocol that Aithon cannot prove automatically.

## Limits are explicit

Runtime checking is not a complete static type proof. Unsupported annotations fail with `UnsupportedType`; they are not silently reduced to `Any`. In particular, Callable signatures, ParamSpec/TypeVarTuple, unregistered Protocols, LiteralString provenance, ReadOnly mutation contracts and arbitrary annotation calls are not automatically proven. Lazy iterator objects supplied by other code are not consumed or wrapped merely to guess their element type; use a typed generator function to check values as they pass yield/send boundaries.

Python object identity and side effects are preserved. A failed mutation check does **not** roll back `append`, an external API call, a property setter, or arbitrary native code. Foreign code/threads are not instrumented internally; Aithon checks its own boundaries. Objects can be temporarily invalid before the next boundary check. For a guarantee that invalid values can never enter an object, a different object model or isolation boundary is needed.

`TypeViolation` and `UnsupportedType` are TypeError subclasses and Aithon contract errors. Ordinary Python contract failures stop without asking AI to reinterpret the declared type. Errors identify the value path (for example `analysis['severity']`) and uncaught errors include the project filename/line.

See [the incident example](../examples/project/main.py) and [the type safety tests](../tests/test_type_safety.py).
</file>

