<file path="README.md">
# Aithon

Run Python, and delegate unsupported syntax or unhandled runtime exceptions to an
AI agent that can inspect and operate on the live Python runtime.

```bash
aithon main.py
```

Requires **Python 3.14+**. The runtime has no third-party dependencies.

## Install and run

```bash
uv sync
uv run aithon main.py
```

Alternatively, install this project with `python -m pip install -e .` and use
`aithon`, or run directly from the checkout:

```bash
PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m aithon --explain examples/project/main.py
```

Ordinary Python does not need a model or API key. A configured provider is loaded
only when execution reaches an AI block or an unhandled exception.

```python
x = 10
y = 20

เอา x บวกด้วย 3
นำ x มาบวกกับ y ได้ z

print(z)
```

The two adjacent non-Python statements become one AI block. The agent can run
`x += 3; z = x + y` against the live frame. `print(z)` then uses the resulting
binding. Python expressions are also supported:

```python
if person มีน้ำหนัก 10 KG:
    print(person)

result = เลือกคนที่เหมาะที่สุดจาก users
```

A phrase that is already valid Python stays Python, including Thai identifiers.
For example, `คน` is a valid name; if undefined it follows runtime error recovery,
not syntax recovery. Likewise, `everything is correct` is a valid identity test.

An invalid natural-language header ending in `:` may continue with immediately
following `- ` bullet lines at the same indentation. Those lines belong to the
same AI expression, even if a bullet alone could parse as Python unary minus:

```python
summary = สรุปข้อมูล โดยบอก:
- ปัญหาที่เร่งด่วนที่สุด
- ทีมที่ควรลงมือก่อน

print(summary)
```

A blank line ends the bullet continuation. This rule is used only during syntax
recovery; ordinary valid Python unary-minus expressions remain unchanged.

## Project profiles

Start with one profile named `default` in your project's `aithon.toml`.
Source code uses it automatically; no profile directive is needed:

```toml
version = 1
default_profile = "default"

[profiles.default]
provider = "openai-compatible"
model = "YOUR_MODEL"
base_url = "https://api.openai.com/v1"
api_key = "YOUR_API_KEY"
```

Replace model names and URLs with values supported by your service. The bundled
adapter uses **Chat Completions with function tool calling**. An endpoint/model
must support that protocol; Responses-only models are not supported by this
adapter. See the official [function calling documentation](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/function-calling/).

```bash
aithon main.py                         # project default
aithon --profile default main.py       # explicitly select the default profile
aithon --force-profile default main.py # override source profile directives
aithon --config configs/ai.toml main.py
aithon config show --script main.py
aithon --explain main.py               # parse/inspect; no program or AI execution
aithon --stats main.py                 # per-invocation measurements on stderr
aithon main.py --my-program-argument   # passed through in sys.argv
```

`--profile` and `--force-profile` are mutually exclusive. Options for Aithon go
before the script; arguments after it belong to the script. To change a project's
default permanently, edit `default_profile`.

Add profiles later by appending another table to the same file:

```toml
[profiles.quality]
provider = "openai-compatible"
model = "YOUR_QUALITY_MODEL"
base_url = "https://api.openai.com/v1"
api_key = "YOUR_API_KEY"
prompt = "ตรวจสอบสมมติฐานก่อนแก้ข้อมูล"
```

Then use `aithon --profile quality main.py`, or add
`# aithon: profile="quality"` before a specific statement. Existing code keeps
using `default` unless you select another profile. Profile names are
case-sensitive, and a profile must be defined before it can be selected.

Configuration selection:

1. An explicit `--config` selects exactly that file.
2. Otherwise, search upward from the script directory for the nearest
   `aithon.toml`, stopping at the Git root (inclusive) or filesystem root.
3. Only if there is no project config, use the optional global config:
   `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/aithon/config.toml` (default `~/.config/aithon/config.toml`)
   on Linux, `~/Library/Application Support/aithon/config.toml` on macOS, or
   `%APPDATA%/aithon/config.toml` on Windows.

Project and global profiles **do not merge**. An invalid project config reports an
error instead of falling back. Imports use the running project's configuration.
The config directory is the project root; with global/no config, the script
directory is the root.

Paste your key directly into `api_key`. Alternatively, use
`api_key_env = "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"` to read it from an environment variable.
If both are supplied, a nonempty `api_key` takes precedence. Neither `config show`
nor configuration object representations display the direct key.

For OpenRouter, set `base_url = "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"` and use your
OpenRouter model ID. `provider` stays `"openai-compatible"`.

An optional `env_file = ".env"` at the top of the config loads credentials from
a file relative to the config directory. Process environment values take
precedence over that file. Environment files accept literal `KEY=value`,
JSON double-quoted strings, or single-quoted strings; they do not execute shell
syntax or expand variables. Aithon does not load an unspecified `.env`, mutate
the process environment, or print credentials in `config show`. Keep `.env` out
of Git. Each project can choose different variable names and credential files.

Profile fields: `provider` and `model` are required; `base_url` defaults to
`https://api.openai.com/v1`; `api_key`, `api_key_env` and `prompt` are optional. `timeout`
defaults to 60 seconds per HTTP request; `max_rounds` defaults to 16 model turns
per agent invocation. There is no automatic HTTP retry or provider fallback.
`tool_choice` defaults to `"required"` because Aithon completes work through tools;
set `"auto"` only for an endpoint that does not support requiring tool calls.
See [OpenRouter's tool-choice documentation](https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/features/tool-calling#tool-choice-configuration).

## Source directives

Use full-line comments. Directives inside strings or inline comments are ignored.
Values are double-quoted strings with JSON escapes.

```python
# aithon: prompt="ให้น้ำหนักกับประสบการณ์มากกว่าจำนวนใบรับรอง"
# aithon: prompt="อธิบายเหตุผลไว้ใน object ผลลัพธ์ด้วย"
result = เลือกคนที่เหมาะที่สุดจาก users
```

Consecutive annotation comments attach to the next statement or AI block at the
same indentation. Blank lines and ordinary comments are allowed. Annotating a
function, class, or compound statement also covers its body and decorators.

These prompt-only directives work with the single `default` profile. After adding
`quality` as shown above, you can also select it for a region:

```python
# aithon: begin profile="quality" prompt="รักษาหน่วยเงินเป็นบาท"
ปรับข้อมูลราคาให้เป็นรูปแบบเดียวกัน
summary = สรุปยอดจาก orders

# aithon: begin profile="default" prompt="ใช้ข้อความสั้น"
description = เขียนคำอธิบายจาก summary
# aithon: end

ตรวจสอบ summary ว่าตรงกับ orders
# aithon: end
```

Regions may nest, but each begin/end pair must stay in one suite at the same
indentation. A point annotation cannot cross a region boundary. Unknown fields,
duplicate profiles, orphan annotations, and unmatched regions are errors before
execution. Profiles are resolved only when AI is needed.

Selection order: `--force-profile` → nearest source directive → `--profile` →
config `default_profile`. Prompts concatenate in this order: selected profile's
prompt, outer-to-inner source context, then the point annotation. Forcing a
profile still retains source prompts. Prompts are literal hints, not executable
templates or changes to the runtime tool protocol.

Directives do not trigger AI for otherwise successful Python. A directive between
two invalid statements separates their AI blocks even if their profiles match.
Functions retain their lexical directives when called elsewhere.

## Runtime exception recovery

Python's `try/except`, context managers, and `finally` execute first. If an
exception reaches an entry-script checkpoint, the agent receives the exception,
traceback, source, and access to live objects. Each top-level executable statement
is a checkpoint; a compound statement such as a loop or `try` is one whole
checkpoint. Module docstrings and future imports retain their compiler treatment.

For example, an agent may supply a replacement value for `result = work()` after
`work()` fails. It can also repair state and explicitly retry that statement, or
re-raise the error. For complex assignments and compound statements it must do
the replacement work through runtime tools before completing recovery.

The profile/prompt comes from the deepest mapped source location in the project
traceback. For dependency errors this is the project's call site. Execution
resumes after the entry-script checkpoint, not inside an unwound function.
Traceback frames may be inspected, but cannot be resumed. Side effects are not
rolled back. Retrying a compound checkpoint repeats its earlier side effects.

There are at most two recovery attempts per checkpoint. Provider/configuration
failures do not recursively invoke recovery. Ctrl+C, `SystemExit`,
`GeneratorExit`, asyncio/futures cancellation, and groups containing such events
propagate unchanged. Process crashes and OS termination cannot be recovered.

## Execution boundaries

- CPython is the grammar oracle. A token-based recovery search tries small
  expressions first and grows to enclosing statements when needed. Ambiguous or
  heavily damaged source may become one large AI block, including valid Python
  inside that block. `--explain` shows exactly what the agent will receive.
- Expression candidates are bounded to 100 tokens per logical statement; larger
  damaged statements use structural fallback. Natural language has no unique
  parse, so this conservative recovery is not a guarantee of intended semantics.
- Live bindings and object mutations use Python 3.14 frame locals. AI-created
  names can be read later in that same invocation. They do not create new lexical
  closure bindings retroactively. Existing static-local, global, and nonlocal
  rules remain in effect.
- Aithon reserves names beginning with `__aithon_`. Runtime instrumentation adds
  bookkeeping to module locals and traceback frames; exact reflection/bytecode
  identity with an uninstrumented CPython process is not promised.
- AI runs synchronously with the process's permissions. Runtime tools are not a
  sandbox. Only providers you configure receive context, and only when AI is
  called. An explicit `eval`/`exec` can perform arbitrary Python operations.
- Project source imports use the same recovery pipeline. Valid modules retain
  Python's normal loader; transformed modules do not write bytecode caches.
  Dependencies and Aithon itself are excluded from source transformation.

## Add a provider

Install an ordinary Python package in the project's environment with this entry
point (no global installation is required):

```toml
[project.entry-points."aithon.providers"]
my-service = "my_package.provider:create_provider"
```

```python
def create_provider(*, profile, api_key):
    return MyProvider(profile, api_key)

class MyProvider:
    def __init__(self, profile, api_key):
        self.profile = profile
        self.api_key = api_key

    def complete(self, messages, tools):
        # Translate to your service and back to an assistant message:
        # {"role": "assistant", "content": None, "tool_calls": [
        #   {"id": "unique-id", "type": "function", "function": {
        #     "name": "evaluate", "arguments": '{"code": "x + y"}'}}
        # ]}
        raise NotImplementedError
```

Select `provider = "my-service"` in a profile. The adapter only communicates with
the model; Aithon's agent executes runtime tools serially and validates their
arguments. Return unique tool-call IDs and JSON-string arguments. Raise
`aithon.models.ProviderError` with a credential-free message for known failures.

Tools are `evaluate`, `execute`, `get_binding`, `set_binding`, `inspect`, `frames`, `get_frame_code`,
and terminal `finish` (syntax) or `recover` (exception). Handles refer to original
Python objects, allowing a model to return objects without JSON-copying them.

## Fewer model round trips

Aithon keeps `evaluate` (return a value) separate from `execute` (side effects and
bindings). A model can finish simple work in **one response** by batching tools:

```text
execute(id="change", code="x += 3; z = x + y")
finish(id="done")
```

For a Python result, the agent can evaluate and return it in a single tool call:

```text
finish(code="__import__('math').pi * float(r)**2")
```

This uses the live frame, checks execution permissions and output types, and
preserves object identity. It avoids a second model response just to return an
evaluation's handle. Provider response time still determines actual latency.

Alternatively, the terminal tool can reference a preceding evaluation's call ID:

```text
evaluate(code="x + y", result_id="answer")
finish(result_from="answer")
```

Expression blocks (assignment values, conditions, arguments and return values)
set `requires_result: true` in the agent request, even without type annotations.
They require `finish(code=...)`, `finish(handle=...)` or `finish(result_from=...)`. A bare `finish()`
rejects the entire batch before any tools run, allowing the agent to correct it.
Writing an assignment through `execute` does not return that value to Python.
An intentional `None` remains valid: evaluate `None` and explicitly return its
result (or use `finish(code="None")`). Standalone statements can still use a bare `finish()` for side effects.

These illustrate function tool calls in one assistant response. `result_id` is
an optional tool argument the model chooses itself, so it need not predict
provider-generated tool-call IDs. `result_from` accepts that name or an actual
earlier call ID for compatibility, and may reference only an earlier `evaluate`
or `get_binding` in the **same batch**. Result names must be unique and must not
collide with call IDs. Returning an already
known `handle` still works, but `code`, `handle` and `result_from` cannot be combined.
Recovery also supports `recover(action="complete", result_from="answer")`.
When a replacement target exists, `recover(action="complete", code="expression")`
evaluates and returns the replacement in one tool call, with the same execution
permissions and type checks. This also works for natural-language identifiers
that parse as Python but raise `NameError` at runtime.
`retry` and `reraise` accept neither value field.
Recovery values are rejected during batch validation if the checkpoint has no
single-name assignment target, before earlier tools can cause side effects.

For the Chat Completions adapter, `--stats` includes `token_usage` when the
provider reports it: prompt/completion totals, reasoning tokens and cached prompt
tokens. These counts accumulate across responses; reasoning and cached tokens
are subsets, not additional totals. Missing counts mean unreported, not zero.
Use these alongside `model_call_seconds` to investigate provider latency.

Every batch is validated before any runtime tool executes. There may be at most
one terminal call, and it must be last. Invalid arguments/dependencies result in
error/skipped responses for the entire batch with no tool execution. Invalid,
duplicate, or reused call IDs stop with a provider protocol error. Corrected
batches must use new IDs. If a tool fails during execution, remaining calls are
skipped and the model can repair the error; completed side effects remain and
are never automatically replayed.

Initial object descriptions include `handle`, `type`, and bounded structural hints,
without raw values. Python dunder names are omitted; redundant module/qualname
fields are omitted from initial metadata. Aliases share the same handle
within an invocation. Metadata does not call user `repr`, properties, or custom
attribute hooks. Exact built-in dictionaries expose up to 20 short string keys
and value types; lists/tuples expose size, up to three sampled item types, and
the first dictionary item’s keys/types when available. Samples are labeled and
are not whole-container type guarantees. Declared annotations, project class
fields and function parameter/return hints come from registered source/scope
metadata without evaluating annotations. Raw contents are inspected only on request. Use
`inspect(handle=..., depth=2)` to inspect a list of dictionaries in one round.
Depth is limited to 1–3 (default 1), with at most `limit` entries per container
(default 20, maximum 100), a traversal budget, and cycle detection. Truncated
collections are marked explicitly.

The model receives `code_context` instead of a full `frame_code`: nearby source
with filename, absolute start/end lines, and a `truncated` flag. Each preview
covers the statement and 12 surrounding lines on each side, capped at 12,000
characters; the separate `statement` remains complete. Recovery includes active
and origin previews. `get_frame_code()` retrieves full registered active-frame
source; `get_frame_code(frame="origin")` retrieves the recovery origin. Unavailable
source is reported explicitly, without reading arbitrary files. Custom agents
still receive the full `AgentRequest.frame_code` for compatibility.

To measure your configured provider:

```bash
uv run aithon --stats examples/project/main.py
```

Stats report model-call count, executed-tool count (including terminal calls),
provider time, runtime-tool time, and cumulative HTTP JSON request bytes for each
invocation, on stderr. They contain no prompts, source, object values or API keys.
`empty_responses`, `invalid_batches`, `tool_failures`, and `tool_counts` explain
extra rounds; `model_call_seconds` shows each provider call's duration.
Bytes are **not tokens**. The bundled adapter reports actual serialized body
bytes; plugins without byte reporting show `null`, not an estimated wire size.
`Provider.complete(messages, tools)` is unchanged. Embedded callers can use
`run_script(..., stats=True)`.

The deterministic tests demonstrate two model calls becoming one for the same
expression result and a substantially smaller initial payload for a large frame.
Actual elapsed speed depends on the model/provider; complex work can still need
several rounds. This optimization does not cache AI results or change models.

For deterministic embedding/tests, `aithon.cli.run_script(path, config=...,
agent_factory=...)` accepts an agent implementing `execute(request, runtime)` and
`recover(request, runtime)`. Recovery returns a `RecoveryDecision`. See
`examples/fake_demo.py` and the tests.

## Tests

```bash
PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v
PYTHONPATH=src python3 examples/fake_demo.py
```

The suite uses fake agents and mocked HTTP, and does not require credentials or
make paid requests. To opt into the real provider smoke test, set
`AITHON_INTEGRATION_CONFIG` to an absolute config path with a default profile,
then rerun the suite. No live provider test runs by default.

## AI capabilities

Aithon also supports project-scoped capability routes for document search, embeddings,
reranking, vision, speech, image generation/editing and video, using OpenRouter,
Gemini and a local SQLite vector index. Existing version 1 profiles still work.

See [the capability runtime guide](docs/capabilities.md) and
[the single-profile configuration example](examples/capabilities/aithon.toml.example).
Use `--trace-plan` to inspect actual routes/cache/timing; `--explain` remains static.

## Type safety

Explicit annotations are checked across ordinary project Python and AI execution:
assignments, arguments/returns, typed fields, mutable containers and generators.
AI sees a schema derived from the same contract, including TypedDict fields,
Literal choices and Annotated descriptions. Unsupported contracts fail explicitly.
See [type safety and its runtime boundaries](docs/type-safety.md).

### Prompt caching for loops

Every semantic invocation still calls the AI with current runtime state. Aithon
places deterministic context (instructions, tools, source context/revision, hints,
types and statement) before a separate live-state message. Objects, including history, are described only by handle and type metadata.
Values stay in the live runtime; the agent can evaluate targeted expressions or
request bounded inspection when needed. No Python strategy or AI answer
is reused, and model/reasoning settings are unchanged.

For OpenRouter, Aithon supplies a deterministic `session_id` based on the project,
profile/model, stable context and tools to improve provider affinity. DeepSeek
and other implicitly cached models need no additional cache flag. Anthropic models
through OpenRouter receive an explicit ephemeral breakpoint on the stable context,
not on changing runtime state. Unknown compatible endpoints receive ordinary
messages without vendor-specific fields. Native explicit Gemini cache resources
are not implemented; compatible endpoints can use their implicit prefix caching.

Run `uv run aithon --stats main.py` and inspect `token_usage.cached_tokens`,
`uncached_prompt_tokens`, `cache_write_tokens`, and `reasoning_tokens` when reported.
Uncached input is derived only when both prompt and cached counts are reported.
Stable local prefixes do not prove a remote cache hit: provider support, minimum
prefix size, expiry and routing affect hits. Caching can reduce input processing
but does not skip new reasoning or output generation. No live latency improvement
is claimed without measurement.

Provider behavior follows the [OpenRouter prompt caching documentation](https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/best-practices/prompt-caching).

### On-demand codebase context

The agent can call `list_code_files(query, offset, limit)`,
`search_code(query, path, limit)` and `read_code(path, start_line, end_line)`
when the current frame lacks context. This works in syntax and recovery modes;
ordinary Python and simple AI expressions do not require a search. Search is
literal (not regex), with project-relative paths and line numbers. Reading a
module does not import or execute it. The agent may then use discovered helpers
through the existing runtime tools.

These tools use the configured project root and require `read_code`, enabled in
default profiles. Version 2 profiles with explicit permission lists must include
`read_code` to allow them. They exclude hidden paths, common dependency/build
folders, symlinks, and file types outside the source/documentation allowlist
(`.py`, `.pyi`, `.md`, `.rst`, `.txt`, `.js`, `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.jsx`, `.sql`).
Configuration files such as `aithon.toml`, JSON credentials and `.env` are excluded.
This is a fixed policy, not a `.gitignore` interpreter or a secret scanner:
credentials embedded in allowed source files are not automatically redacted.

Reads are limited to 512 KB files, 200 lines and 16,000 characters per call;
long individual lines are marked as truncated. Searches scan up to 8 MB of source
and 10,000 filesystem entries, returning bounded matches and explicit truncation
or skipped-file counts. Narrow a search or read a specific path for more context.
Search results are not cached locally, so subsequent searches see file changes.
This permission controls these tools only; `execute_code` remains trusted Python
execution with process privileges, not a filesystem sandbox.

### Source files are not AI repair targets

AI syntax execution and recovery must operate on live runtime objects and return
values; natural-language source is intentional and must never be rewritten.
During tool dispatch and runtime eval/exec, a Python audit guard rejects ordinary
filesystem writes, truncation, removal and replacement of source files, including
symlinks resolving to source. It also rejects subprocess launches/native library
loads and directory moves/removal during those operations. Failures are returned
as `SourceWriteError` so the agent can correct its runtime action. This restriction
applies even when `write_filesystem` is enabled. Normal Python outside AI tool
execution is unaffected; non-source output files remain writable.

The guard is defense against accidental agent edits, not a hostile-code sandbox:
pre-opened writable handles, existing native extensions, and background threads
can bypass Python audit coverage. Full adversarial isolation requires a separate
OS-level execution boundary and is not claimed here.
